WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.164
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:08.816
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.116 --> 00:00:16.273
Unit twenty-nine: Review: Thinking About Thinking. Today you will reflect on reasoning using emphatic inversion.

4
00:00:16.573 --> 00:00:19.260
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.328
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:25.370
Metacognition.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:30.182
Metacognition means awareness and regulation of one's own thinking.

8
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:34.385
Metacognition helps students notice when they have misunderstood.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:37.845
Your turn. Say: Metacognition.

10
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:41.840
Metacognition.

11
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:43.550
Reflection.

12
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.059
Reflection means careful thought about one's experience and beliefs.

13
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.485
Reflection turns experience into understanding.

14
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:54.515
Your turn. Say: Reflection.

15
00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:58.356
Reflection.

16
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.164
Assumption.

17
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.264
Assumption means something taken to be true without proof.

18
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.314
Every argument rests on some assumption.

19
00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:11.068
Your turn. Say: Assumption.

20
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:14.865
Assumption.

21
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.432
Reasoning.

22
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:21.123
Reasoning means the process of thinking logically to reach a conclusion.

23
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:24.761
Good reasoning is transparent and open to correction.

24
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.700
Your turn. Say: Reasoning.

25
00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:31.452
Reasoning.

26
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:33.218
Bias.

27
00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:37.981
Bias means a systematic tendency to favour one view unfairly.

28
00:01:38.583 --> 00:01:41.953
Confirmation bias leads us to seek only agreeing evidence.

29
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.202
Your turn. Say: Bias.

30
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:48.940
Bias.

31
00:01:49.683 --> 00:01:50.847
Perspective.

32
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.616
Perspective means a particular way of viewing something.

33
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:58.377
Seeking another perspective exposes blind spots.

34
00:01:58.983 --> 00:02:02.193
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

35
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:06.209
Perspective.

36
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:07.974
Clarity.

37
00:02:08.983 --> 00:02:12.132
Clarity means the quality of being clear and precise.

38
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:15.459
Clarity of thought precedes clarity of speech.

39
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.609
Your turn. Say: Clarity.

40
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:22.305
Clarity.

41
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:23.935
Wisdom.

42
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.993
Wisdom means sound judgment based on knowledge, experience and reflection.

43
00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:32.430
Wisdom includes knowing the limits of one's knowledge.

44
00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:35.263
Your turn. Say: Wisdom.

45
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.043
Wisdom.

46
00:02:39.833 --> 00:02:43.310
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:48.564
Visal, at the end of this course I realise how often my first opinion was wrong.

48
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:56.668
That is progress. Only by reflecting can we recognise our biases, since they operate invisibly.

49
00:02:57.783 --> 00:02:58.959
Is there a method?

50
00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:08.658
Several. Only when assumptions are stated can they be tested, and not until we seek another perspective do our blind spots appear.

51
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.202
So thinking about thinking is the real skill.

52
00:03:13.317 --> 00:03:21.205
Indeed. Rarely do we change our minds without effort, and never has wisdom come to those who refuse to doubt.

53
00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:23.925
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.783 --> 00:03:27.874
Grammar focus: Emphatic inversion (Only by).

55
00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.349
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.885
Only by reflecting can we recognise our biases.

57
00:03:33.783 --> 00:03:36.584
Only when assumptions are stated can they be tested.

58
00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.957
Not until we seek another perspective do our blind spots appear.

59
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:52.761
A negative or limiting phrase at the start forces inversion: Only by…, Only when…, Not until…, Rarely…, Never… + auxiliary + subject. Do-support if there is no auxiliary.

60
00:03:53.517 --> 00:03:58.387
Be careful! Don't say: Only by reflecting we can recognise bias.

61
00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:04.109
Say: Only by reflecting can we recognise bias.

62
00:04:04.409 --> 00:04:07.256
Only + phrase → invert the auxiliary.

63
00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:10.533
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.333 --> 00:04:12.787
Thinking About Thinking.

65
00:04:13.087 --> 00:04:18.744
Metacognition, awareness of one's own thinking, is arguably the most transferable intellectual skill.

66
00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:26.539
Psychologists have documented systematic biases, such as confirmation bias, that distort reasoning without our noticing.

67
00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:32.435
Only by reflecting on our assumptions can we begin to see how they steer our conclusions.

68
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:38.753
Seeking other perspectives helps, for only when views are challenged do our weaknesses become visible.

69
00:04:39.767 --> 00:04:46.294
Never has wisdom been identified with certainty; rather, it consists in the humility to keep questioning what we think we know.

70
00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.506
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.406
Only by reflecting blank we recognise our biases.

72
00:04:55.706 --> 00:04:59.348
A, can. B, we can. C, could to.

73
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:04.574
The answer is A: can.

74
00:05:05.717 --> 00:05:08.726
Awareness and regulation of one's own thinking is blank.

75
00:05:09.026 --> 00:05:12.622
A, metacognition. B, bias. C, wisdom.

76
00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:18.340
The answer is A: metacognition.

77
00:05:19.483 --> 00:05:22.377
Something taken to be true without proof is an blank.

78
00:05:22.677 --> 00:05:26.504
A, assumption. B, perspective. C, clarity.

79
00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:32.066
The answer is A: assumption.

80
00:05:33.217 --> 00:05:34.972
Choose the correct inversion.

81
00:05:35.272 --> 00:05:41.689
A, Rarely we change our minds.. B, Rarely do we change our minds.. C, Rarely we do change minds..

82
00:05:45.233 --> 00:05:48.649
The answer is B: Rarely do we change our minds..

83
00:05:49.783 --> 00:05:52.700
According to the reading, wisdom consists in…

84
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.965
A, certainty. B, humility to keep questioning. C, avoiding doubt.

85
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.382
The answer is B: humility to keep questioning.

86
00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:08.869
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:09.717 --> 00:06:11.496
How do you check your own reasoning?

88
00:06:28.283 --> 00:06:29.644
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:29.944 --> 00:06:55.868
I check my reasoning by stating my assumptions, looking for evidence that could prove me wrong and asking someone with a different perspective to challenge it. Only by exposing an argument to criticism can I judge whether it is sound. I also wait before deciding when I feel strongly, since emotion can disguise bias. Reflection is slow, but it saves me from costly errors.

90
00:06:56.168 --> 00:06:57.970
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:58.883 --> 00:07:03.579
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and emphatic inversion (only by).

92
00:07:03.879 --> 00:07:08.993
Today's value is metacognitive awareness. Think about your own thinking to grow wiser.

93
00:07:09.293 --> 00:07:16.046
Congratulations! You have finished Online C two, Book two. See you in Online C two, Book three!

94
00:07:16.346 --> 00:07:19.416
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

